[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. It holds three problems.
The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not close #4471. The other two problems keep coming in after the fix,
and each one now has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Tenants that write the day
  first get the day and month swapped: "the calendar invite for 7 April landed on 4 July".
  This is the bug #4471 names.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first
  tenants on booking-v3 type month first, as the hint says, and the form reads the date
  their way. The parser fix does not reach it: the fix is the hint.
- **`one-day move`**: the date moves by one day instead of swapping: "Tuesday 6 October
  showed as Monday", "client in Lisbon got Tuesday, client in Toronto got Monday". A swap
  cannot do that. It looks like a timezone bug. The desk noted these reports, but its
  table counts them under `parser`.

## How the reports were placed

Each report is placed by its tenant and the way it came in, with `causes.ts`, not by its
subject line or by intake's tag. Intake tags from the reporter's browser, and its tag
disagrees with the cause for some of these reports. The table below is the desk's own
view, with that count:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Every report text was read as well. All `parser` and `hint` reports describe a day and
month swap, or no date at all. The reports in `one-day move` are the only ones that
describe a one-day shift.

## Why one ticket held three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. These three
problems share the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so the rule
merged them. Its consequence was written for the other case, the same bug under different
subjects. It does not say what happens when different bugs share a subject.

## Lessons

- A subject says what the reporter saw, not what broke. Before you mark a big ticket known,
  check that the fix you queue explains every report in it.
- Count reports by id. The ticket export repeats some rows.

